Good call. Step one: find out whether pgsi_ans00315015 has the rds_iam role granted.

This is the highest-value check because if it does, no amount of secret-wiring will fix DEV — Aurora will keep rejecting the proxy's password login for that user, which is exactly the PAM error you're seeing.

Connect to Aurora as kfb_aurora_admin (bastion, psql, or whatever you normally use) and run:

sql
SELECT r.rolname AS member, g.rolname AS granted_role
FROM pg_auth_members m
JOIN pg_roles r ON r.oid = m.member
JOIN pg_roles g ON g.oid = m.roleid
WHERE r.rolname = 'pgsi_ans00315015';

Three possible outcomes:

rds_iam appears in the results → that's your culprit. We revoke it and set a password.
Query returns zero rows → the user exists but has no role grants; rds_iam isn't the issue and we move to the secret.
You get nothing because the role doesn't exist → different problem entirely, and worth knowing now.

To cover that last case, run this too:

sql
SELECT rolname, rolcanlogin, rolvaliduntil
FROM pg_roles
WHERE rolname = 'pgsi_ans00315015';

Paste back what both queries return and we'll take the next step from there.

